# Overrides for the local development stack (`docker-compose.yml`).
#
# ADR: adr-0031-compose-roles-and-credentials.md, adr-0037-compose-build-provenance.md
# Produced by: TASK-059
#
# ONE VARIABLE HAS NO DEFAULT ANYWHERE, and it is set here, first, rather than left to
# look like the ones below it: BETTER_AUTH_SECRET (ADR-0051) is a JWT signing key, so
# nothing is committed for it. `docker compose up` fails at parse time, naming the
# variable, until you supply one — either export it yourself:
#
#   export BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="$(docker run --rm node:24-alpine node -e 'console.log(require("node:crypto").randomBytes(32).toString("base64url"))')"
#
# — or uncomment the line below with a generated value and copy this file to `.env`.
# Either way, `scripts/check-compose-stack.sh` (`pnpm test:compose`) needs nothing from
# you: it generates and exports its own value for the duration of its own run. But a
# project-root `.env` costs that script something the export does not — while `.env`
# exists at the repository root, `pnpm test:compose` REFUSES TO RUN AT ALL ("cannot run
# the AC-115 check: there is a .env at the repository root", exit 2, nothing measured),
# because a root `.env` masks the same defect an exported variable would. Move it aside
# before running that command.
#
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
#
# EVERY VARIABLE FROM HERE DOWN has a default in the compose file, so `docker compose up`
# on a fresh clone needs none of them. Copy this file to `.env` only when you want to
# change one.
#
# Compose reads `.env` at the project root for interpolation. It is gitignored: this
# repository is public, so anything secret that lands here is rotated rather than deleted.
#
# One thing to know before you set any of the four passwords. The roles are created ONCE,
# by /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d against an empty data directory. Changing a password here
# after the first `up` changes nothing: the stack keeps the old roles, the new DSN does not
# match them, and the failure is an authentication dead end with no obvious cause. Run
# `docker compose down -v` first, which destroys the database.

# The bootstrap superuser's password. Nothing in the stack authenticates as it: every
# statement the API, the migrator and the seed issue comes from one of the three
# NOBYPASSRLS roles below, because a superuser is exempt from every RLS policy.
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=postgres

# shortkit_migrator owns every table and can run any DDL, including DROP. It is the
# largest of the three credentials.
SHORTKIT_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=migrator

# shortkit_app is the runtime role. It owns nothing and holds no BYPASSRLS; the API
# refuses to boot if it does.
SHORTKIT_APP_PASSWORD=app

# shortkit_auth is Better Auth's role and nothing else connects as it (ADR-0050). It
# owns nothing and holds no BYPASSRLS, same as shortkit_app, and it is the role that
# will hold `jwks.privateKey` once TASK-002's migration lands — rotate this one too,
# not just the two above.
SHORTKIT_AUTH_PASSWORD=auth

# THE THREE PASSWORDS ABOVE MUST BE URL-SAFE. `DATABASE_URL`, `DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL`
# and `DATABASE_AUTH_URL` are assembled by string interpolation in `docker-compose.yml`,
# and a DSN has no escaping mechanism the way psql's `:'variable'` form does. A `@`, `/`,
# `:` or `?` breaks the URL and the error names neither this file nor the character. The
# roles script itself quotes correctly whatever you put here; the DSNs are the constraint.

# The commit the API image reports at GET /health. Unset, `docker-compose.yml` builds with
# git's null object id — forty zeros — which says "built by docker compose, provenance
# unknown" (ADR-0037). Setting it HERE is not the documented way and is a trap: the value
# is stale the moment you commit anything. The documented form supplies it per invocation:
#
#   GIT_COMMIT_SHA="$(git rev-parse HEAD)" docker compose up --build
#
# GIT_COMMIT_SHA=

# Compose derives the project name from the top-level `name:` in `docker-compose.yml`,
# which is `shortkit-dev`. Set this to give a second clone its own containers, network and
# volume; it overrides the pinned name (precedence: `-p`, then this, then `name:`, then the
# directory basename). Without it, two clones share `shortkit-dev_pgdata` whatever their
# directories are called.
#
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=
